---
/**
 * The only page with personal content, and the only one written in a
 * conversational register.
 *
 * SOURCING RULE, unchanged from the page's first version: every factual line
 * here traces to something Ryan supplied. What is deliberately ABSENT is every
 * specific he did not — tenures, dates, titles, degree, major, team names,
 * follower counts, times and placings. Those are the details that make an about
 * page good and also the ones that get invented to complete a rhythm, so the
 * prose is shaped to read naturally without them.
 *
 * PR #59's ruling 4 said no session writes biography into this page. Ryan
 * supplying the facts in the founder-site-v2 task prompt is what lifts that, and
 * only for those facts — see the dated reversal in docs/decisions.md.
 *
 * One thing here is a CORRECTION, not an addition: the previous copy read "I
 * started in enterprise development — at CED, and then at Prudential", which put
 * CED in the past and ordered it first. CED is the current day job; Prudential
 * came before it.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { AMENITY_BRAND, EMAIL_DEV, FULL_NAME, PAGES, REGION } from '../site';

/**
 * ---- EDIT THE CAPTIONS HERE. This is the only place they live. ----
 *
 * The "Off the clock" row. Three photos, deliberately not four: three reads as
 * a considered row and four starts reading as a feed, which is the failure mode
 * for a personality row on a credibility page.
 *
 * `caption` is Ryan's to rewrite freely — one line each, and the register to
 * match is the rest of this page: dry, plain, no exclamation marks. `alt` is
 * NOT interchangeable with it. Alt text describes the picture for someone who
 * cannot see it, so it stays literal and stays in sync with the image; a
 * caption that merely repeats the alt earns nothing.
 *
 * SOURCING RULE, same as the prose above: nothing here names a place, a date or
 * an event that Ryan did not supply. The photos show a museum courtyard, a
 * roadside statue and a park trail, and that is exactly as specific as the
 * captions get — the location names are guessable from the images and guessing
 * is how invented facts get onto this page.
 *
 * Adding a fourth: drop the file in `public/off-the-clock/`, process it the way
 * the others were (4:5, 480×600, EXIF stripped — phone photos carry GPS), and
 * add an entry. The CSS row reflows on its own.
 *
 * 480×600 is MEASURED, not chosen: the rendered box is 176 CSS px at Lighthouse's
 * mobile emulation and 221 px on desktop, so 480 covers the widest case at that
 * device's DPR with nothing spare. The first cut of this row shipped 600×750 and
 * Lighthouse's `uses-responsive-images` scored it 0.5 for ~286 KiB of waste.
 */
const OFF_THE_CLOCK = [
  {
    src: '/off-the-clock/trail.jpg',
    alt: `${FULL_NAME} on a wooded park trail, wearing earbuds.`,
    caption: 'The boring part of progress. Most of it looks like this.',
  },
  {
    src: '/off-the-clock/museum.jpg',
    alt: `${FULL_NAME} mirroring the pose of a gilded archer statue in a museum courtyard.`,
    caption: 'A museum courtyard, and a technique that needs work.',
  },
  {
    src: '/off-the-clock/rooster.jpg',
    alt: `${FULL_NAME} squaring up to an oversized painted rooster statue outside a red barn.`,
    caption: 'Some roadside attractions earn the stop.',
  },
];

/**
 * Intrinsic size of every file in the row, emitted as `width`/`height` on each
 * `<img>`. All three are processed to exactly this, so the browser reserves the
 * right box before the bytes arrive and the row cannot shift the page as it
 * loads. This site measures CLS 0 and images are the classic way to lose it.
 */
const OFF_THE_CLOCK_W = 480;
const OFF_THE_CLOCK_H = 600;
---

<BaseLayout page={PAGES.about} ogAlt={`About ${FULL_NAME}`}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="h1">The longer version</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The rest of this site is about work. This page is about how I got to it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--plain">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">The day job</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I am a full-stack developer at <strong>CED</strong> — Consolidated Electrical
          Distributors — where I build internal software for warehouse operations. React and
          TypeScript on the front, C# and .NET behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Warehouse software is a good teacher because the people using it are not impressed by
          it. Nobody opens a picking screen because they enjoy the picking screen. It either
          makes the next hour of their shift easier or it is in the way, and you find out which
          within a day of shipping. That is a much shorter feedback loop than most software
          careers give you, and it has made me suspicious of anything that demos well and works
          badly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">Before that</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I studied at <strong>Rowan University</strong>, and did software development at
          <strong>Prudential</strong> before CED. Big systems, long release trains, code that
          other people's jobs depended on. It is not a glamorous way to learn, but it is a
          thorough one: you find out quickly that most software problems are really
          communication problems, and that the person who can explain a tradeoff in plain
          language is worth more than the person who can only implement it.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a stretch I also taught STEM part-time at <strong>Mad Science</strong>. If you want
          to know whether you actually understand something, try explaining it to a room of
          children who will tell you immediately and without tact when you have lost them. I
          have never had a client meeting that was harder than that, and I have never since
          believed that jargon is the same thing as expertise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">Before that: an audience</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Earlier on I spent a long stretch in competitive gaming, and a good chunk of it
          streaming and making content. It reads like an odd line on a founder page until you
          consider what it actually is: you go live, in real time, with no edit button, and
          either people stay or they leave. You learn to hold attention, to read a room you
          cannot see, and to be unbothered when something breaks on camera.
        </p>
        <p>
          I use that more than I expected to. A cold call with a property manager and a
          three-hour stream have the same underlying problem — you have a very short window to be
          worth listening to, and no amount of preparation substitutes for being at ease.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">Three sports, one habit</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Track and field, basketball and soccer. Track taught me the thing I am most grateful
          for: that progress is mostly boring, and that the work you do on a day you do not feel
          like it is the work that counts. Basketball and soccer taught the opposite half — that
          you can be the fittest person on the pitch and still lose because you would not pass.
        </p>
        <p>
          I still train. It is the only reliable way I have found to keep several things running
          at once without getting brittle about any of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">Off the clock</h2>
      <ul class="offclock" role="list">
        {
          OFF_THE_CLOCK.map((shot) => (
            <li>
              <figure class="offclock__figure">
                <img
                  class="offclock__img"
                  src={shot.src}
                  width={OFF_THE_CLOCK_W}
                  height={OFF_THE_CLOCK_H}
                  alt={shot.alt}
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
                <figcaption class="offclock__caption">{shot.caption}</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell stack">
      <h2 class="h2 tick">What I am building now</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Four things, all out of {REGION}, all outside the day job.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>site-factory</strong> — fast, honest websites for local businesses. Most of the
          small-business web near me is either a decade-old page that does not work on a phone or
          a template on a subscription nobody can cancel. There is a lot of room between those.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AI voice agents</strong> — the practical end of the AI wave. A dental practice
          that misses calls at 6pm does not need a chatbot on its website; it needs the phone
          answered. That is a solvable problem now, and it was not two years ago.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>{AMENITY_BRAND}</strong> — curated smart markets in luxury residential
          buildings. This one came from noticing that the amenity floor of a very nice building is
          often the least considered part of it, and that the fix costs the building nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Styled by Alhambra</strong> — an AI-powered personal styling app, built in
          React Native and Expo, and the closest of the four to launch. It is the one that is
          least like the others: consumer rather than local, a product rather than a service, and
          a much harder problem than it looks from the outside.
        </p>
        <p>
          If any of that is useful to you, or you just want to compare notes, I am at{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL_DEV}`}>{EMAIL_DEV}</a>. I answer my own email.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
